ngs of the colonization of Mars, from the arrival of the First Hundred to the First Martian Revolution.","edition_count":37,"first_publish_year":1992,"goodreads_ids":["6607231","71311","https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/25766392-red-mars","41133","41140","977461","832173","41140","6607231","41133","832173","977461","211365","211308","77507","2961973","211308","2961972"],"key":"/works/OL81665W","languages":["chi","fre","spa","ita","und","eng"],"match":"title","ol_already_read":105,"ol_currently_reading":6,"ol_rating":3.631579,"ol_rating_count":76,"ol_readinglog_count":302,"ol_want_to_read":190,"pages_median":592,"source":"openlibrary","title":"Red Mars","url":"https://openlibrary.org/works/OL81665W"}],[74,{"amazon_asins":["B00OVLMRG4"],"cover_url":"https://covers.openlibrary.org/b/id/7890719-L.jpg","description":"This is Christopher's murder mystery story. There are no lies in this story because Christopher can't tell lies. Christopher does not like strangers or the colours yellow or brown or being touched. On the other hand, he knows all the countries in the world and their capital cities and every prime number up to 7507. When Christohper decides to find out who killed the neighbour's dog, his mystery story becomes more complicated than he could ever have predicted.","edition_count":108,"first_publish_year":2002,"first_sentence":"It was 7 minutes after midnight.","goodreads_ids":["895993","26171450","213361","821527","1618","38748049","406622","228044","3466690","152878","618259","2269018","406622","6360085","3439","26171450","10749758","895993"],"key":"/works/OL2965102W","languages":["fre","spa","pol","tur","dut","heb","ita","ger","vie","chi","eng","por","epo"],"match":"identifier","ol_already_read":351,"ol_currently_reading":93,"ol_rating":3.946281,"ol_rating_count":242,"ol_readinglog_count":1767,"ol_want_to_read":1323,"pages_median":256,"source":"openlibrary","title":"The Curious Incident of the Dog in the Night-Time","url":"https://openlibrary.org/works/OL2965102W"}],[144,{"amazon_asins":["B019B6WTVQ"],"cover_url":"https://covers.openlibrary.org/b/id/8184979-L.jpg","description":"\"Like Paul Farmer in Mountains Beyond Mountains, Paul English grew up poor, in working-class Boston, but as Tracy Kidder writes, he had \"a mind for the age that was coming.\" Brilliant, reckless, endlessly energetic, Paul English, after Kayak sold for $2 billion, asked himself: What comes next? Start another company? Use his new wealth to make a difference in the world? With a riveting, page-turning narrative and unmatched storytelling skill, Kidder casts a fresh and critical eye on how new technologies and start-ups, new money, are reshaping our culture\"--","edition_count":3,"first_publish_year":2016,"key":"/works/OL17876078W","languages":["eng"],"link_titles":["New York Times review"],"link_urls":["http://www.nytimes.com/2016/10/16/books/review/tracy-kidder-truck-full-of-money.html"],"match":"identifier","ol_already_read":1,"ol_currently_reading":0,"ol_readinglog_count":6,"ol_want_to_read":5,"pages_median":259,"source":"openlibrary","title":"A Truck Full of Money","url":"https://openlibrary.org/works/OL17876078W"}],[164,{"amazon_asins":["855651121X","B08GB58KD5","B08FHBV4ZX"],"cover_url":"https://covers.openlibrary.org/b/id/11200092-L.jpg","description":"Ryland Grace is the sole survivor on a desperate, last-chance mission–and if he fails, humanity and the earth itself will perish. Except that right now, he doesn’t know that. He can’t even remember his own name, let alone the nature of his assignment or how to complete it. All he knows is that he’s been asleep for a very, very long time. And he’s just been awakened to find himself millions of miles from home, with nothing but two corpses for company.\r\n\r\nHis crewmates dead, his memories fuzzily returning, he realizes that an impossible task now confronts him. Alone on this tiny ship that’s been cobbled together by every government and space agency on the planet and hurled into the depths of space, it’s up to him to conquer an extinction-level threat to our species.\r\n\r\nAnd thanks to an unexpected ally, he just might have a chance.\r\n\r\nPart scientific mystery, part dazzling interstellar journey, *Project Hail Mary* is a tale of discovery, speculation, and survival to rival *The Martian*–while taking us to places it never dreamed of going.","edition_count":32,"first_publish_year":2021,"first_sentence":"\"What's two plus two?\"","form":"novel","goodreads_ids":["58033783","56049167","59656600","58014357","58033840","61026000","57946654","222697645","54493402","54906250"],"key":"/works/OL21745884W","languages":["dut","spa","fin","por","tur","kor","eng","fre","ger"],"link_titles":["Book page on author's web site","New York Times review","Starmap of the locations mentioned in Project Hail Mary"],"link_urls":["https://www.andyweirauthor.com/books/project-hail-mary","https://www.nytimes.com/2021/05/04/books/review/andy-weir-project-hail-mary.html","https://valhovey.github.io/gaia-mary/"],"main_subjects":["extraterrestrial life","solar luminosity","cowardice","global catastrophic risk","ice age"],"match":"identifier","ol_already_read":297,"ol_currently_reading":43,"ol_rating":4.531579,"ol_rating_count":190,"ol_readinglog_count":1456,"ol_want_to_read":1116,"original_language":"English","pages_median":496,"source":"openlibrary","title":"Project Hail Mary","url":"https://openlibrary.org/works/OL21745884W","wikidata":"Q106852836","wikidata_publication_date":"2021-05-04","wikidata_url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q106852836"}],[202,{"amazon_asins":["972232988X","3453315944","B0169AU2ZC","8576571846","B00YBA7PGW","B01NCU4VNT","B01NCU4VNT"],"cover_url":"https://covers.openlibrary.org/b/id/10618463-L.jpg","description":"[Comment by Kim Stanley Robinson, on The Guardian's website](https://www.theguardian.com/books/2011/may/14/science-fiction-authors-choice):\r\nThe Left Hand of Darkness by Ursula K Le Guin (1969)\r\n\r\n> One of my favorite novels is The Left Hand of Darkness, by Ursula K Le Guin. For more than 40 years I've been recommending this book to people who want to try science fiction for the first time, and it still serves very well for that. One of the things I like about it is how clearly it demonstrates that science fiction can have not only the usual virtues and pleasures of the novel, but also the startling and transformative power of the thought experiment.\r\n\r\n> In this case, the thought experiment is quickly revealed: \"The king was pregnant,\" the book tells us early on, and after that we learn more and more about this planet named Winter, stuck in an ice age, where the humans are most of the time neither male nor female, but with the potential to become either. The man from Earth investigating this situation has a lot to learn, and so do we; and we learn it in the course of a thrilling adventure story, including a great \"crossing of the ice\". Le Guin's language is clear and clean, and has within it both the anthropological mindset of her father Alfred Kroeber, and the poetry of stories as magical things that her mother Theodora Kroeber found in native American tales. This worldly wisdom applied to the romance of other planets, and to human nature at its deepest, is Le Guin's particular gift to us, and something science fiction will always be proud of. Try it and see – you will never think about people in quite the same way again.","edition_count":92,"first_publish_year":1969,"first_sentence":"I'll make my report as if I told a story, for I was taught as a child on my homeworld that Truth is a matter of the imagination.","form":"novel","goodreads_ids":["6956433","234698","6665775","6665775","161131","224280","4338325","965878","965882","118028","6994530","224279","118028","224278","965881","831877","6135995","965880","18423","365704","118028","831874","2508460","831875","118028","68028","831880","92587","2195304","2674648","429952"],"key":"/works/OL59800W","languages":["fre","cat","spa","rus","ita","por","eng","ger","tur","heb","kor"],"match":"identifier","ol_already_read":110,"ol_currently_reading":25,"ol_rating":4.2586207,"ol_rating_count":58,"ol_readinglog_count":718,"ol_want_to_read":583,"original_language":"English","pages_median":304,"source":"openlibrary","title":"The Left Hand of Darkness","url":"https://openlibrary.org/works/OL59800W","wikidata":"Q2008211","wikidata_publication_date":"1969-03-01","wikidata_url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q2008211"}]]},{"labels":["Author"],"typed":{},"rows":[[8,{"amazon_author_id":"B001ILIG4C","bio":"American productivity consultant and author","birth_date":"28 December 1945","goodreads_id":"1058","image_url":"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/TNW%20Con%20EU15-David%20Allen%20-%204.jpg","isni":"0000000114754047","key":"/authors/OL2628117A","languages":["English"],"name":"David Allen","occupations":["writer","author","adviser","blogger","consultant"],"source":"openlibrary","url":"https://openlibrary.org/authors/OL2628117A","viaf":"76646248","wikidata":"Q178369","wikidata_birth_date":"1945-12-28","wikidata_url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q178369","wikipedia_url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_Allen_(author)"}],[9,{"birth_date":"1945 Dec. 28","key":"/authors/OL6353310A","name":"David Allen","personal_name":"David Allen","source":"openlibrary","url":"https://openlibrary.org/authors/OL6353310A"}],[10,{"key":"/authors/OL423096A","name":"David Allen","personal_name":"Allen, David.","source":"openlibrary","url":"https://openlibrary.org/authors/OL423096A"}],[40,{"alternate_names":["Robinson, Kim Stanley","Robinson, K. S.","Kim S. Robinson","K. S. Robinson","Ким Стенли Робинсон","金·史丹利·羅賓遜","金·斯坦利·鲁宾逊","金·斯坦利·罗宾逊","キム・スタンリー・ロビンソン","キム・スタンリー・ロビンスン","Kim Stenli Robinson"],"amazon_author_id":"B000APVJXC","bio":"American science fiction writer","birth_date":"23 March 1952","goodreads_id":"1858","image_url":"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Kim%20Stanley%20Robinson.jpg","isni":"00